      "d_strain_z (strain Frobenius, z-scored; == raw strain_frob)": 0.6684
    },
    "FINDING": "The 3-component equal-weight descriptor specified in the task is NOT predictive of model error (Spearman +0.068). Component-wise: strain-Frobenius carries essentially all of the signal (+0.668); the shape/rattle term is ANTI-correlated (-0.054) because the rattle family has the largest shape-RMSD and the smallest error (0.149 meV/atom); and the volume term AS Z-SCORED is inert (+0.0225), because mean-centring places its minimum at the training mean rather than at zero deviation. The strong +0.687 sometimes quoted for volume belongs to abs(dvol) on the RAW, un-mean-centred scale - it is NOT the z-scored component, and conflating the two was an error in an earlier draft of this report. PROVENANCE OF +0.709: that figure is form 'v3' - the 2-component (volume + strain) distance scaled by the TRAIN RMS of each component measured FROM THE RELAXED REFERENCE, with no mean-centring, so the reference structure sits at distance zero. It is reproducible and, by a 4000-sample bootstrap, genuinely higher than strain alone (mean difference +0.041, 95% CI [+0.017, +0.066]). It is NOT the same object as the mean-centred 2-component form 'v2', which scores only +0.227. ADOPTED: strain-Frobenius alone (+0.668). The +0.041 gain from v3 is small, and it is bought with a second fitted scale parameter; strain alone already captures isotropic volume change analytically (for F = sI, ||F-I||_F = sqrt(3)|s-1|), so the extra term is largely redundant. The screening tool uses strain alone.",
